From 8ca2d461a64ccbe88cb157fbf1513685fc48a866 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dom Cobley <popcornmix@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 21:38:24 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 2019/2064] Resolution: select a native 3D display mode for
 stereoscopic playback

The display mode search only logged is3D, so a sink was never switched into
a hardware 3D mode. Frame packing reports its packed scanout (1920x2205) and
lost on size to plain 1080p; no 3D mode ever reached the candidate list,
because the default whitelist's "no smaller than the current mode" floor
excludes every 1080- and 720-line HDMI 3D mode from a 4K desktop and a user
whitelist never names one; and the search demanded the layout the source
used, though HDMI 1.4 mandates only frame packing and half top-and-bottom at
24p. 3D content played as two half-width images on a display that was never
told to expect them.

Choose the mode for the output arrangement instead:

- Match modes against one eye. A full side-by-side or top-and-bottom frame
  is halved on its long axis; a half packing already describes one eye.

- Let a whitelisted 2D timing license the sink's 3D variants of it: they
  are the same timing transported differently, so nobody has to whitelist
  1920x2205. Frame packing's 2D geometry is recovered through iBlanking.
  Measure the default whitelist's floor against one eye, and when no 3D
  mode is as large as one eye (any 4K stereoscopic source) fall back to
  the smaller ones rather than lose 3D. Drop the 3D modes from the
  whitelist setting's options, since they are no longer a choice;
  videoscreen.resolution still lists them for manual selection.

- Output the split arrangement the display can carry. SPLIT_VERTICAL
  needs a side-by-side mode, SPLIT_HORIZONTAL a top-and-bottom or frame
  packing one, and the renderer crops the correct eye either way. So
  SetStereoMode() asks ChooseStereoArrangement() which of the two has the
  better mode - earlier refresh tier, then frame packing over a half mode
  - and applies that, if the render system supports it. A display with
  no native 3D mode is left alone.

- For stereo output pick the largest whitelisted mode natively flagged
  with the matching layout, trying the content rate, double it, 3:2
  pulldown, then the desktop rate. These tiers are not gated on the
  whitelist's double-rate and pulldown settings: 3D EDIDs carry sparse
  rates and the alternative is usually no 3D mode at all.

- For 2D output, which includes "Watch as 2D", erase the natively 3D
  modes from the candidates, and if the mode already set is a 3D one fall
  back to the desktop mode (when that is 2D), so 2D content is never left
  in a mode the sink splits. Mask the stereo bits out of the MODEMASK
  comparisons and measure the floor against the desktop mode:
  GetResInfo() ORs the current GUI stereo mode onto every mode it
  returns, so those comparisons cannot tell a native 3D mode from a 2D
  one.

- Decide against the requested stereo mode (GetNextStereoMode(), added
  here) rather than the current one: SetStereoMode() takes effect a flip
  later, and the search is triggered by the change itself.

- Re-run the search when the stereo mode changes during playback, by
  asking the player, which owns the stream parameters.

- Change nothing when refresh rate adjusting is off. The mode is then the
  one picked by hand in videoscreen.resolution, and remapping the
  arrangement for a mode that is never going to be switched to only
  splits the frame the wrong way for the one that is in use.

The stereoscopicpreferframepacking advanced setting (default true) turns
the size preference around, which is the only way to reach the half modes
on a display that offers frame packing at every timing that has one.

Other windowing backends are unaffected: none flags a mode MODE3DSBS or
MODE3DTB, and Windows stereo uses RenderStereoMode::HARDWAREBASED.

(cherry picked from commit 928c82100348965fa4c760f6a307ea9488231267)
